2025-11-01 21:44:05,782 - hybrid_agentic_analysis - INFO - combined result: {'tracker': <dynamodb_tracker.DynamoDBTracker object at 0x10c4c6390>, 'input_config': InputConfig(project_name='Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices'), 'project_config': ProjectConfig(project_name='Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices', project_id='870ebb7e-237b-4e31-b219-1f8f016b3b56', result_path=''), 'project_result': None, 'combine_phase': False, 'research_data': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1\r\nDate: March 15, 2024\r\nInterviewer: Research Team Member\r\nInterviewee: Alex Morrison (Pet Parent)\r\nDuration: 45 minutes\r\nPets: Agent Dale Cooper (Cat, 6 years old) and Audrey Horne (Dog - Golden Retriever, 4 years old)\r\n\r\nQuestion 1: Can you tell me about your overall approach to keeping Agent Dale Cooper and Audrey Horne healthy? What does pet wellness mean to you?\r\nResponse: Oh, that\'s such a big question to start with! For me, pet wellness is really about looking at the whole picture, you know? It\'s not just about making sure they\'re not sick - it\'s about them thriving and being happy. With Agent Dale Cooper and Audrey, I think about it in layers. There\'s the physical health aspect, obviously - making sure they\'re at a good weight, their coats are shiny, they\'re active. But there\'s also the mental and emotional side that I think people sometimes overlook.\r\nAgent Dale Cooper, being a cat, he needs different things than Audrey does. He needs mental stimulation through play, places to climb and perch, windows to look out of. He\'s very particular about his environment being just right. Audrey, on the other hand, she needs that social interaction, the physical exercise, the training that keeps her mind engaged.\r\nI guess my overall approach is preventative rather than reactive. I\'d rather invest time and energy into keeping them healthy than dealing with problems after they arise. That means regular vet visits, yes, but also daily observation. I know their normal behaviors so well that I can tell immediately if something\'s off. Like last month, Agent Dale Cooper was just sitting differently - nothing dramatic, but I could tell his posture was off. Turned out he had a minor muscle strain from jumping weird off the cat tree.\r\nI also believe in balance. I\'m not one of those pet parents who goes to extremes - I don\'t cook elaborate meals from scratch every day or anything like that. But I do research everything I give them, every toy I buy, every change I make to their routine. It\'s about making informed decisions that work for our lifestyle while prioritizing their wellbeing.\r\n\r\nQuestion 2: Let\'s talk about diet specifically. What do you feed Agent Dale Cooper and Audrey, and how did you decide on their current feeding routines?\r\nResponse: Diet has been quite a journey for us, actually. When I first got Agent Dale Cooper five years ago, I just bought whatever cat food was on sale at the grocery store. I didn\'t know any better. But then he started having some digestive issues - nothing serious, but enough that the vet suggested I look into higher quality food. That sent me down this whole rabbit hole of pet nutrition research.\r\nFor Agent Dale Cooper now, I feed him a combination of high-quality wet and dry food. In the morning, he gets about 3 ounces of wet food - I rotate between three different brands to give him variety. Right now, I\'m using brands that focus on high protein content with no fillers or by-products. The first ingredient is always actual meat, not meal or by-product. He gets salmon-based food twice a week because it\'s good for his coat, and chicken or turkey the other days. I tried duck once, but he looked at me like I\'d personally offended him and refused to eat it for two days.\r\nThroughout the day, he has access to dry food, but I measure it out - about 1/4 cup total. I learned the hard way that free-feeding without limits led to him gaining two pounds in three months. The vet wasn\'t happy about that. His dry food is a dental health formula because cats are prone to dental issues, and honestly, trying to brush Agent Dale Cooper\'s teeth is like trying to negotiate with a tiny, furry tornado.\r\nAudrey\'s diet is different because she\'s much more active and, being a Golden Retriever, she has different nutritional needs. She gets fed twice a day - morning and evening. I give her about 1.5 cups of high-quality dry kibble each meal, so 3 cups total per day. Her food is specifically formulated for active adult dogs, with glucosamine for joint health since Goldens can have hip issues later in life.\r\nI supplement Audrey\'s diet with healthy treats during training - things like small pieces of carrot, green beans, or these little freeze-dried liver treats that she goes absolutely crazy for. But I count those calories as part of her daily intake. I learned to do that after she gained five pounds one summer when I was doing a lot of training with her and didn\'t account for all those treats.\r\nOh, and water! I\'m obsessive about water. They both have multiple water stations around the house, and I change the water twice a day. Agent Dale Cooper has this fancy ceramic fountain because cats prefer moving water, apparently. Audrey just has regular bowls, but I have one inside and one outside because she\'s always running in and out.\r\nThe timing of meals is important too. Agent Dale Cooper gets his wet food at 7 AM and 6 PM like clockwork. If I\'m even five minutes late, he sits by his bowl and gives me this look of utter betrayal. Audrey eats at 7:30 AM and 5:30 PM. I feed them at slightly different times to avoid any food competition issues, even though they respect each other\'s bowls pretty well.\r\n\r\nQuestion 3: How do you handle exercise and physical activity for each of your pets? What does a typical day look like?\r\nResponse: Exercise routines are completely different for each of them, as you can imagine! Audrey is my marathon runner, and Agent Dale Cooper is more of a sprinter who believes in strategic energy conservation.\r\nLet me start with Audrey because her routine is more structured. We begin every single day with a morning walk, rain or shine. I wake up at 6 AM, and by 6:30, we\'re out the door. The morning walk is usually about 45 minutes to an hour, depending on the weather and my schedule. We have three different routes we rotate through to keep things interesting for her. One goes through the park where she can sniff all the good smells, another goes past the lake where she sometimes gets to swim in summer, and the third is more of an urban route where we practice her leash manners around distractions.\r\nAfter work, around 5 PM, before dinner, we do another activity. Three days a week, this is another walk, usually 30 minutes. But twice a week, we go to the dog park instead, where she can really run and play with other dogs for about an hour. Weekends are when we do our big adventures - hiking trails, beach trips if the weather\'s nice, or sometimes agility practice in the backyard. I set up some basic agility equipment - nothing fancy, just some jumps and weave poles - and she loves it.\r\nBeyond structured exercise, Audrey gets mental stimulation through training sessions. We do about 10-15 minutes of training every evening. Sometimes it\'s basic obedience reinforcement, sometimes we\'re learning new tricks. Right now, we\'re working on "tidy up," where she puts her toys back in the toy basket. She\'s getting pretty good at it, though she sometimes tries to cheat by just dropping them near the basket rather than in it.\r\nAgent Dale Cooper\'s exercise needs are totally different. Cats need short bursts of intense activity rather than sustained exercise. I do two dedicated play sessions with him daily - one in the morning after breakfast and one in the evening before bed. Each session is about 15-20 minutes, or until he starts panting or loses interest. I use different toys to simulate hunting behaviors - feather wands for bird-like prey, laser pointers for quick movement (though I always end with a physical toy so he can actually "catch" something), and these little fake mice that he likes to carry around.\r\nThe interesting thing about Agent Dale Cooper is that he\'s most active at dawn and dusk - classic cat behavior. So around 5 AM, I often hear him racing around upstairs, doing what I call his "morning zoomies." He runs from room to room, jumps on and off furniture, and generally acts like he\'s possessed for about 10 minutes. Then he\'s done and goes back to sleep. He does the same thing around 9 PM.\r\nI\'ve also set up the house to encourage natural movement for him. I have cat trees in three different rooms, shelves mounted on the walls to create climbing paths, and window perches where he can watch birds. He probably climbs up and down his various perches 20-30 times a day. I\'ve actually watched him sometimes, and he has favorite routes through the house that involve jumping from surface to surface without touching the floor - like the floor is lava or something.\r\nOne challenge is that Agent Dale Cooper is an indoor-only cat, so I have to be creative about exercise. I\'ve trained him to walk on a harness and leash, and we go out in the backyard sometimes, but he\'s not really into it. He mostly just sits and eats grass and glares at me for subjecting him to the indignity of a harness. But the indoor play sessions seem to be enough for him - he maintains a healthy weight and the vet says his muscle tone is good.\r\n\r\nQuestion 4: Can you walk me through your veterinary care routine? How often do you take them in, and what does preventive care look like for you?\r\nResponse: Veterinary care is something I take really seriously - probably to the point where my vet jokes that I\'m keeping them in business! But I\'ve seen what happens when people skip preventive care, and it\'s both heartbreaking and expensive.\r\nBoth Agent Dale Cooper and Audrey go in for annual wellness exams, but at different times of the year. I deliberately scheduled them six months apart - Agent Dale Cooper goes in June, Audrey in December - partly to spread out the cost, but also because this way I\'m thinking about pet health stuff throughout the year rather than just once.\r\nThe annual visits are comprehensive. They include a full physical exam, updating vaccinations, blood work, urinalysis, and fecal testing for parasites. For Agent Dale Cooper, we also do a dental check because he\'s starting to show some tartar buildup. The vet has mentioned we might need to do a dental cleaning under anesthesia next year, which I\'m not looking forward to - both because I worry about him under anesthesia and because it\'s expensive.\r\nAudrey gets a few extra things at her annual visit. We do heartworm testing every year, even though she\'s on preventive medication year-round. We also started doing hip and elbow evaluations when she turned three, just to establish a baseline since Golden Retrievers can be prone to dysplasia. So far, everything looks great, but I want to catch any issues early.\r\nBeyond the annuals, I take them in for sick visits when needed. I\'ve probably averaged 2-3 extra visits per year between the two of them. Last year, Agent Dale Cooper had a urinary issue that required two visits and follow-up testing. The year before that, Audrey got into something on a hike and had severe diarrhea that needed treatment. I don\'t mess around with "wait and see" if something seems genuinely wrong. I\'d rather pay for an unnecessary vet visit than miss something serious.\r\nPreventive care goes beyond just vet visits though. Both pets are on monthly flea and tick prevention year-round. People sometimes say I don\'t need to do it in winter, but our vet disagrees, and I trust them. Agent Dale Cooper uses a topical treatment that I apply between his shoulder blades. Audrey takes an oral chewable that she thinks is a treat, which makes my life easier.\r\nAudrey is also on heartworm prevention monthly - it\'s actually a combination medication that also covers intestinal parasites. Agent Dale Cooper doesn\'t need heartworm prevention since he\'s indoor-only, but I do give him a dewormer twice a year just to be safe.\r\nI keep detailed health records for both of them at home. I have a binder with all their vaccine records, test results, weights from each visit, notes about any issues. I also keep a calendar where I mark when I give preventive medications, when they\'re due for things, any symptoms I notice. It might seem excessive, but it\'s helped me catch patterns. Like, I noticed Agent Dale Cooper tends to have more hairballs in spring, so now I start giving him hairball remedy preemptively in March.\r\nOh, and I maintain relationships with both a regular vet and an emergency vet. Our regular vet is great for routine stuff and minor illnesses, but they\'re only open until 6 PM weekdays and half days on Saturday. The emergency vet is 24/7 but way more expensive. I\'ve had to use them three times - once when Audrey ate chocolate (she was fine, but scary), once when Agent Dale Cooper was straining to urinate on a Sunday, and once when Audrey cut her paw pad on broken glass during an evening walk.\r\nI also believe in asking questions at vet visits. I come prepared with a list. Things like whether their food is still appropriate, if there are new vaccines or treatments I should know about, what health issues I should watch for as they age. The vet probably thinks I\'m neurotic, but they\'re always patient with me.', 'chunks': [{'id': '1', 'content': 'Pet wellness is about the whole picture, not just preventing sickness but ensuring pets thrive and are happy.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'quote', 'confidence': 0.9, 'tags': ['pet wellness', 'holistic approach']}, {'id': '2', 'content': 'Alex Morrison practices a preventative approach to pet care, focusing on regular vet visits and daily observation.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['preventative care', 'observation']}, {'id': '3', 'content': 'Agent Dale Cooper (cat) and Audrey Horne (dog) have different needs for mental and physical health.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'observation', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['species differences', 'mental health', 'physical health']}, {'id': '4', 'content': 'Alex Morrison believes in balance and making informed decisions about pet care, avoiding extremes.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.9, 'tags': ['balanced approach', 'informed decisions']}, {'id': '5', 'content': 'Diet for Agent Dale Cooper includes high-quality wet and dry food, with variety and attention to nutritional content.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'fact', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['diet', 'cat nutrition']}, {'id': '6', 'content': "Audrey's diet is formulated for active adult dogs, with glucosamine for joint health and calorie-counted treats.", 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'fact', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['diet', 'dog nutrition']}, {'id': '7', 'content': 'Alex Morrison emphasizes the importance of water, providing multiple water stations and changing water twice a day.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['hydration', 'water stations']}, {'id': '8', 'content': "Exercise routines are tailored to each pet's species and personality, with structured activities and mental stimulation.", 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'observation', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['exercise', 'mental stimulation']}, {'id': '9', 'content': 'Annual wellness exams for pets include physical exams, vaccinations, blood work, and specific tests like dental checks or hip evaluations.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'fact', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['veterinary care', 'annual exams']}, {'id': '10', 'content': 'Preventive care includes monthly flea and tick prevention, heartworm prevention for Audrey, and maintaining detailed health records.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['preventive care', 'health records']}, {'id': '11', 'content': 'Alex Morrison keeps a binder with all vaccine records, test results, and notes about any health issues for both pets.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'fact', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['health records', 'organization']}, {'id': '12', 'content': 'Alex Morrison has relationships with both a regular vet and an emergency vet, emphasizing the importance of being prepared for any situation.', 'source': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['veterinary care', 'emergency preparedness']}], 'inferences': [{'chunk_id': '1', 'meanings': ['Pet wellness encompasses both physical health and emotional well-being, aiming for a holistic approach to care.', 'The concept of pet wellness extends beyond mere disease prevention to include the overall quality of life for pets.'], 'importance': "Understanding pet wellness in a comprehensive manner is crucial for providing care that addresses all aspects of a pet's life, not just their physical health.", 'context': 'This highlights a shift in pet care philosophy towards a more integrated approach that considers both the physical and emotional needs of pets.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The statement implies a holistic view of pet health, suggesting that true wellness involves more than just avoiding illness. It reflects a broader trend in pet care that values mental and emotional health alongside physical health.'}, {'chunk_id': '2', 'meanings': ['Alex Morrison adopts a proactive strategy in pet care, emphasizing the importance of routine veterinary check-ups and daily monitoring of pet health.'], 'importance': 'This approach is significant because it can lead to early detection of health issues, potentially saving lives and reducing long-term healthcare costs.', 'context': 'This tells us that preventative care is becoming a more recognized and essential part of responsible pet ownership, focusing on maintaining health rather than just treating illness.', 'confidence': 0.9, 'reasoning': 'By focusing on regular vet visits and observation, Alex Morrison is taking steps to prevent health issues before they become serious, illustrating the value of preventative care in pet health management.'}, {'chunk_id': '3', 'meanings': ['Different species of pets, like cats and dogs, have unique requirements for their mental and physical well-being.'], 'importance': 'This distinction is important because it underscores the need for pet owners to understand and cater to the specific needs of their pets, which can vary widely between species.', 'context': 'This indicates a growing awareness of the diverse needs of pets, moving away from a one-size-fits-all approach to pet care.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': "The mention of different needs for a cat and a dog highlights the complexity of pet care, requiring owners to tailor their approach to each pet's individual requirements."}, {'chunk_id': '4', 'meanings': ['Alex Morrison values a moderate approach to pet care, prioritizing informed choices over following extreme or unfounded practices.'], 'importance': 'This philosophy is crucial as it promotes a balanced and evidence-based approach to pet care, which can lead to better health outcomes for pets.', 'context': 'This reflects a trend towards more nuanced and personalized pet care strategies, moving away from rigid or overly simplistic methods.', 'confidence': 0.9, 'reasoning': 'By advocating for balance and informed decision-making, Alex Morrison is emphasizing the importance of understanding and adapting to the unique needs of each pet, rather than adhering to one-size-fits-all solutions.'}, {'chunk_id': '5', 'meanings': ["Agent Dale Cooper's diet is carefully selected to ensure it is high in quality and meets his specific nutritional needs, incorporating both wet and dry food for variety."], 'importance': 'A diet tailored to the specific nutritional requirements of a pet is essential for maintaining their health and well-being.', 'context': "This highlights the increasing attention to the quality and composition of pet food, recognizing that a well-rounded diet is key to a pet's overall health.", 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': "The focus on high-quality food and nutritional content for Agent Dale Cooper's diet illustrates the importance of a balanced and thoughtful approach to feeding pets, ensuring they get the necessary nutrients for their health."}, {'chunk_id': '6', 'meanings': ["Audrey's diet is specifically designed for her lifestyle as an active adult dog, including supplements for joint health and controlled calorie intake."], 'importance': 'This approach to diet formulation is significant because it addresses the unique health and energy requirements of active dogs, potentially improving their quality of life and longevity.', 'context': 'This indicates a trend towards personalized pet diets that consider the individual activity levels and health needs of pets.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': "By formulating a diet that includes glucosamine and calorie-counted treats, there's a clear focus on supporting Audrey's joint health and managing her energy needs, reflecting a tailored approach to pet nutrition."}, {'chunk_id': '7', 'meanings': ['Alex Morrison places a high priority on hydration, ensuring pets have constant access to fresh water by providing multiple water stations and frequent water changes.'], 'importance': "Hydration is a critical aspect of pet health, and this practice can significantly impact a pet's well-being and prevent health issues related to dehydration.", 'context': 'This emphasizes the growing recognition of the basic yet essential aspects of pet care, such as hydration, in maintaining overall health.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The emphasis on water accessibility and quality for pets shows an understanding of the fundamental needs of animals, highlighting the importance of hydration in their daily care routine.'}, {'chunk_id': '8', 'meanings': ['Exercise routines for pets are customized based on the type of pet and their individual personality, incorporating both physical activities and mental challenges.'], 'importance': 'Personalized exercise plans are crucial for meeting the physical and mental health needs of pets, contributing to their overall well-being and happiness.', 'context': 'This reflects an understanding that pets, like humans, require varied and stimulating activities for their health, indicating a move towards more comprehensive and thoughtful pet care practices.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': "Tailoring exercise routines to each pet's needs and personality acknowledges the diversity among pets and the importance of engaging them in activities that suit their individual preferences and requirements."}, {'chunk_id': '9', 'meanings': ['Annual wellness exams for pets are comprehensive, covering physical assessments, vaccinations, and specific health screenings, such as dental and hip evaluations.'], 'importance': 'These exams are vital for early detection of potential health issues and maintaining overall pet health, highlighting the importance of regular veterinary care.', 'context': 'This indicates a proactive approach to pet health, where preventative measures and early detection play key roles in ensuring the well-being of pets.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The comprehensive nature of annual wellness exams underscores the importance of a thorough approach to monitoring pet health, allowing for early intervention and better management of potential health issues.'}, {'chunk_id': '10', 'meanings': ["Preventive care for pets includes routine measures against common parasites and maintaining up-to-date health records, tailored to each pet's specific needs."], 'importance': 'This proactive approach to health care is essential for preventing common but potentially serious health issues in pets, emphasizing the importance of regular maintenance and documentation.', 'context': 'This highlights the shift towards preventative health measures in pet care, recognizing the value of routine interventions in maintaining pet health and preventing disease.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'By including monthly flea and tick prevention and heartworm prevention for specific pets, along with detailed health records, this approach illustrates a comprehensive strategy for managing and preventing health issues in pets.'}, {'chunk_id': '11', 'meanings': ['Alex Morrison meticulously maintains a binder with all relevant health documentation for each pet, ensuring easy access to their medical history and care details.'], 'importance': 'This organized approach to record-keeping is crucial for effective health management, allowing for quick reference and informed decision-making in care and treatment.', 'context': 'This practice reflects a broader trend towards more diligent and systematic management of pet health information, facilitating better coordinated and informed care.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The detailed maintenance of health records in a binder indicates a commitment to thorough and responsible pet care, ensuring that all information is readily available for reference or in case of emergency.'}, {'chunk_id': '12', 'meanings': ["Alex Morrison's proactive establishment of relationships with both a regular and an emergency vet underscores the importance of preparedness for any health situation that may arise with pets."], 'importance': 'This preparedness is key to ensuring timely and effective medical care for pets in both routine and emergency situations, potentially saving lives.', 'context': 'This approach highlights the growing recognition of the need for comprehensive health care planning for pets, including both preventive care and emergency preparedness.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'By having relationships with both types of veterinary services, Alex Morrison is equipped to handle a wide range of health scenarios, illustrating a proactive and comprehensive approach to pet health care.'}], 'patterns': [{'name': 'Holistic and Proactive Pet Care', 'description': "This pattern highlights the comprehensive approach to pet care, emphasizing both preventive measures and the consideration of pets' physical and emotional well-being.", 'related_inferences': ['1', '2', '3', '4', '7', '8', '9', '10', '12'], 'themes': ['Holistic Care', 'Proactive Measures', 'Physical and Emotional Well-being', 'Preventive Care'], 'strength': 0.9, 'evidence_count': 9}, {'name': 'Customized Nutrition and Health Management', 'description': 'This pattern focuses on the tailored dietary and health management practices for pets, based on their individual needs, lifestyle, and specific health requirements.', 'related_inferences': ['5', '6', '10', '11'], 'themes': ['Customized Diet', 'Health Management', 'Nutritional Needs', 'Lifestyle Consideration'], 'strength': 0.8, 'evidence_count': 4}], 'insights': [{'headline': 'Pets as Family Members Reshape Healthcare Norms', 'explanation': 'The shift towards holistic and proactive pet care reflects a deeper societal change where pets are increasingly viewed as integral family members, deserving of the same level of care and attention as human relatives. This trend challenges the traditional view of pets as mere companions or property, advocating for a more inclusive understanding of family and well-being. It underscores a fundamental truth about the evolving human-animal bond and its impact on consumer behavior, healthcare standards, and ethical considerations in pet care.', 'pattern_id': 'Holistic and Proactive Pet Care', 'non_consensus': True, 'first_principles': True, 'impact_score': 0.9, 'supporting_evidence': ['Rising pet healthcare expenditure', 'Increased adoption of pet insurance', 'Growing demand for pet mental health services']}, {'headline': 'Customized Pet Diets Disrupt Traditional Pet Food Industry', 'explanation': 'The movement towards customized nutrition and health management for pets signals a disruption in the pet food industry, moving away from one-size-fits-all solutions towards personalized offerings. This shift is driven by a deeper understanding of the unique needs of each pet, influenced by genetics, environment, and lifestyle. It challenges the mass production and marketing strategies of traditional pet food companies, highlighting a fundamental shift towards individualized care and the potential for innovation in product development, distribution, and consumer engagement.', 'pattern_id': 'Customized Nutrition and Health Management', 'non_consensus': True, 'first_principles': True, 'impact_score': 0.8, 'supporting_evidence': ['Emergence of pet DNA testing for diet optimization', 'Growth in personalized pet food subscription services', 'Increasing veterinary recommendations for tailored diets']}], 'design_principles': [{'principle': 'The system should treat pets as integral family members, ensuring healthcare services are holistic and proactive.', 'insight_id': 'Pets as Family Members Reshape Healthcare Norms', 'action_verbs': ['treat', 'ensure'], 'design_direction': 'Holistic and proactive healthcare services for pets', 'priority': 0.9, 'feasibility': 0.7}, {'principle': 'The experience must provide personalized nutrition options for pets, reflecting their unique needs and lifestyles.', 'insight_id': 'Customized Pet Diets Disrupt Traditional Pet Food Industry', 'action_verbs': ['provide', 'reflect'], 'design_direction': 'Personalized nutrition and health management for pets', 'priority': 0.8, 'feasibility': 0.6}], 'current_step': 'completed', 'messages': [AIMessage(content='Created 12 chunks using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Generated 12 inferences using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Identified 2 patterns using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Generated 2 insights using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Created 2 design principles using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={})], 'analysis_metadata': {'request_id': '3844c39f-de8b-4a15-8b87-a697431f9603', 'framework': 'Hybrid (LangGraph + OpenAI Agentic)', 'model': 'gpt-4-turbo-preview', 'orchestration': 'LangGraph', 'function_calling': 'OpenAI Native', 'research_data_s3_path': 'research_data/aas-1.txt'}}
