2025-11-06 21:18:45,352 - hybrid_agentic_analysis - INFO - combined result: {'tracker': <dynamodb_tracker.DynamoDBTracker object at 0x110206870>, 'input_config': InputConfig(project_name='Debug-2 Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices'), 'project_config': ProjectConfig(project_name='Debug-2 Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices', project_id='9a1d4b0d-d282-45d3-83fe-2d485aade7a0', result_path=''), 'project_result': None, 'combine_phase': False, 'research_data': 'Interview Transcript: Pet Parent Health and Wellness Practices Part 1\r\nDate: March 15, 2024\r\nInterviewer: Research Team Member\r\nInterviewee: Alex Morrison (Pet Parent)\r\nDuration: 45 minutes\r\nPets: Agent Dale Cooper (Cat, 6 years old) and Audrey Horne (Dog - Golden Retriever, 4 years old)\r\n\r\nQuestion 1: Can you tell me about your overall approach to keeping Agent Dale Cooper and Audrey Horne healthy? What does pet wellness mean to you?\r\nResponse: Oh, that\'s such a big question to start with! For me, pet wellness is really about looking at the whole picture, you know? It\'s not just about making sure they\'re not sick - it\'s about them thriving and being happy. With Agent Dale Cooper and Audrey, I think about it in layers. There\'s the physical health aspect, obviously - making sure they\'re at a good weight, their coats are shiny, they\'re active. But there\'s also the mental and emotional side that I think people sometimes overlook.\r\nAgent Dale Cooper, being a cat, he needs different things than Audrey does. He needs mental stimulation through play, places to climb and perch, windows to look out of. He\'s very particular about his environment being just right. Audrey, on the other hand, she needs that social interaction, the physical exercise, the training that keeps her mind engaged.\r\nI guess my overall approach is preventative rather than reactive. I\'d rather invest time and energy into keeping them healthy than dealing with problems after they arise. That means regular vet visits, yes, but also daily observation. I know their normal behaviors so well that I can tell immediately if something\'s off. Like last month, Agent Dale Cooper was just sitting differently - nothing dramatic, but I could tell his posture was off. Turned out he had a minor muscle strain from jumping weird off the cat tree.\r\nI also believe in balance. I\'m not one of those pet parents who goes to extremes - I don\'t cook elaborate meals from scratch every day or anything like that. But I do research everything I give them, every toy I buy, every change I make to their routine. It\'s about making informed decisions that work for our lifestyle while prioritizing their wellbeing.\r\n\r\nQuestion 2: Let\'s talk about diet specifically. What do you feed Agent Dale Cooper and Audrey, and how did you decide on their current feeding routines?\r\nResponse: Diet has been quite a journey for us, actually. When I first got Agent Dale Cooper five years ago, I just bought whatever cat food was on sale at the grocery store. I didn\'t know any better. But then he started having some digestive issues - nothing serious, but enough that the vet suggested I look into higher quality food. That sent me down this whole rabbit hole of pet nutrition research.\r\nFor Agent Dale Cooper now, I feed him a combination of high-quality wet and dry food. In the morning, he gets about 3 ounces of wet food - I rotate between three different brands to give him variety. Right now, I\'m using brands that focus on high protein content with no fillers or by-products. The first ingredient is always actual meat, not meal or by-product. He gets salmon-based food twice a week because it\'s good for his coat, and chicken or turkey the other days. I tried duck once, but he looked at me like I\'d personally offended him and refused to eat it for two days.\r\nThroughout the day, he has access to dry food, but I measure it out - about 1/4 cup total. I learned the hard way that free-feeding without limits led to him gaining two pounds in three months. The vet wasn\'t happy about that. His dry food is a dental health formula because cats are prone to dental issues, and honestly, trying to brush Agent Dale Cooper\'s teeth is like trying to negotiate with a tiny, furry tornado.\r\nAudrey\'s diet is different because she\'s much more active and, being a Golden Retriever, she has different nutritional needs. She gets fed twice a day - morning and evening. I give her about 1.5 cups of high-quality dry kibble each meal, so 3 cups total per day. Her food is specifically formulated for active adult dogs, with glucosamine for joint health since Goldens can have hip issues later in life.\r\nI supplement Audrey\'s diet with healthy treats during training - things like small pieces of carrot, green beans, or these little freeze-dried liver treats that she goes absolutely crazy for. But I count those calories as part of her daily intake. I learned to do that after she gained five pounds one summer when I was doing a lot of training with her and didn\'t account for all those treats.\r\nOh, and water! I\'m obsessive about water. They both have multiple water stations around the house, and I change the water twice a day. Agent Dale Cooper has this fancy ceramic fountain because cats prefer moving water, apparently. Audrey just has regular bowls, but I have one inside and one outside because she\'s always running in and out.\r\nThe timing of meals is important too. Agent Dale Cooper gets his wet food at 7 AM and 6 PM like clockwork. If I\'m even five minutes late, he sits by his bowl and gives me this look of utter betrayal. Audrey eats at 7:30 AM and 5:30 PM. I feed them at slightly different times to avoid any food competition issues, even though they respect each other\'s bowls pretty well.\r\n\r\nQuestion 3: How do you handle exercise and physical activity for each of your pets? What does a typical day look like?\r\nResponse: Exercise routines are completely different for each of them, as you can imagine! Audrey is my marathon runner, and Agent Dale Cooper is more of a sprinter who believes in strategic energy conservation.\r\nLet me start with Audrey because her routine is more structured. We begin every single day with a morning walk, rain or shine. I wake up at 6 AM, and by 6:30, we\'re out the door. The morning walk is usually about 45 minutes to an hour, depending on the weather and my schedule. We have three different routes we rotate through to keep things interesting for her. One goes through the park where she can sniff all the good smells, another goes past the lake where she sometimes gets to swim in summer, and the third is more of an urban route where we practice her leash manners around distractions.\r\nAfter work, around 5 PM, before dinner, we do another activity. Three days a week, this is another walk, usually 30 minutes. But twice a week, we go to the dog park instead, where she can really run and play with other dogs for about an hour. Weekends are when we do our big adventures - hiking trails, beach trips if the weather\'s nice, or sometimes agility practice in the backyard. I set up some basic agility equipment - nothing fancy, just some jumps and weave poles - and she loves it.\r\nBeyond structured exercise, Audrey gets mental stimulation through training sessions. We do about 10-15 minutes of training every evening. Sometimes it\'s basic obedience reinforcement, sometimes we\'re learning new tricks. Right now, we\'re working on "tidy up," where she puts her toys back in the toy basket. She\'s getting pretty good at it, though she sometimes tries to cheat by just dropping them near the basket rather than in it.\r\nAgent Dale Cooper\'s exercise needs are totally different. Cats need short bursts of intense activity rather than sustained exercise. I do two dedicated play sessions with him daily - one in the morning after breakfast and one in the evening before bed. Each session is about 15-20 minutes, or until he starts panting or loses interest. I use different toys to simulate hunting behaviors - feather wands for bird-like prey, laser pointers for quick movement (though I always end with a physical toy so he can actually "catch" something), and these little fake mice that he likes to carry around.\r\nThe interesting thing about Agent Dale Cooper is that he\'s most active at dawn and dusk - classic cat behavior. So around 5 AM, I often hear him racing around upstairs, doing what I call his "morning zoomies." He runs from room to room, jumps on and off furniture, and generally acts like he\'s possessed for about 10 minutes. Then he\'s done and goes back to sleep. He does the same thing around 9 PM.\r\nI\'ve also set up the house to encourage natural movement for him. I have cat trees in three different rooms, shelves mounted on the walls to create climbing paths, and window perches where he can watch birds. He probably climbs up and down his various perches 20-30 times a day. I\'ve actually watched him sometimes, and he has favorite routes through the house that involve jumping from surface to surface without touching the floor - like the floor is lava or something.\r\nOne challenge is that Agent Dale Cooper is an indoor-only cat, so I have to be creative about exercise. I\'ve trained him to walk on a harness and leash, and we go out in the backyard sometimes, but he\'s not really into it. He mostly just sits and eats grass and glares at me for subjecting him to the indignity of a harness. But the indoor play sessions seem to be enough for him - he maintains a healthy weight and the vet says his muscle tone is good.\r\n', 'chunks': [{'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-1', 'content': 'Pet wellness is about the whole picture, not just preventing sickness but ensuring pets thrive and are happy.', 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'quote', 'confidence': 0.9, 'tags': ['pet wellness', 'holistic approach']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-2', 'content': 'Alex Morrison practices preventative care for pets, focusing on regular vet visits and daily observation to catch issues early.', 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['preventative care', 'observation']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-3', 'content': 'Agent Dale Cooper (cat) requires mental stimulation, places to climb, and windows to look out of.', 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['cat needs', 'mental stimulation']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-4', 'content': 'Audrey Horne (dog) needs social interaction, physical exercise, and engaging training.', 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['dog needs', 'social interaction', 'physical exercise']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-5', 'content': 'Alex Morrison believes in balance and making informed decisions about pet care, avoiding extremes.', 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'quote', 'confidence': 0.9, 'tags': ['balanced approach', 'informed decisions']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-6', 'content': "Agent Dale Cooper's diet includes high-quality wet and dry food, with a focus on high protein content and variety.", 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'fact', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['cat diet', 'high-quality food', 'protein content']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-7', 'content': "Audrey Horne's diet is formulated for active adult dogs, including glucosamine for joint health.", 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'fact', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['dog diet', 'active dogs', 'joint health']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-8', 'content': 'Alex Morrison is attentive to water intake, providing multiple water stations and changing water twice a day.', 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['water intake', 'hydration']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-9', 'content': "Audrey Horne's exercise routine includes morning walks, dog park visits, and agility practice.", 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['dog exercise', 'routine', 'agility practice']}, {'id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-10', 'content': 'Agent Dale Cooper engages in short bursts of intense activity, with dedicated play sessions and natural movement encouraged by home setup.', 'source': 'Interview with Alex Morrison', 'type': 'behavior', 'confidence': 0.95, 'tags': ['cat exercise', 'play sessions', 'home setup']}], 'inferences': [{'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-1', 'meanings': ['Pet wellness encompasses both physical health and emotional well-being.', 'Ensuring pets thrive involves more than just medical care; it includes their happiness and overall quality of life.'], 'importance': 'This perspective shifts the focus from merely treating or preventing illness to promoting a holistic approach to pet care.', 'context': 'It highlights a growing recognition of the complex needs of pets, emphasizing that their well-being is multifaceted.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The statement broadens the definition of wellness to include emotional and mental health, not just the absence of disease, which is crucial for a comprehensive approach to pet care.'}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-2', 'meanings': ['Alex Morrison adopts a proactive approach to pet health, emphasizing early detection and regular monitoring.', 'Preventative care is seen as key to maintaining pet health, involving both professional veterinary care and attentive observation at home.'], 'importance': 'This approach can lead to earlier detection of health issues, potentially reducing the severity of illnesses and improving outcomes for pets.', 'context': 'It reflects a trend towards preventive health measures in pet care, mirroring similar approaches in human healthcare.', 'confidence': 0.9, 'reasoning': "By focusing on regular check-ups and daily monitoring, Alex Morrison's method aims to catch and address health issues before they become serious, illustrating the value of prevention over treatment."}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-3', 'meanings': ['Cats like Agent Dale Cooper have specific environmental needs for mental health, including stimulation and opportunities for natural behaviors.', "Providing varied physical environments and visual stimuli is essential for a cat's mental well-being."], 'importance': "Understanding and meeting these needs can prevent behavioral issues and improve a cat's quality of life.", 'context': 'This highlights the importance of environmental enrichment in pet care, recognizing that pets have complex needs beyond basic physical health.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The mention of specific needs such as mental stimulation and physical structures for climbing suggests that catering to the natural instincts and behaviors of pets is crucial for their overall well-being.'}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-4', 'meanings': ['Dogs like Audrey Horne require a combination of social, physical, and mental engagement for their well-being.', "Active and engaging training is as important as exercise and social interaction for a dog's health."], 'importance': 'This underscores the multifaceted nature of dog care, where physical activity, mental stimulation, and socialization are all crucial.', 'context': 'It reflects a comprehensive understanding of dog behavior and needs, emphasizing that well-rounded care contributes to a healthier, happier pet.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The statement points out that dogs thrive on a balance of activities that cater to their physical, mental, and social needs, indicating that neglecting any one aspect can impact their overall health and happiness.'}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-5', 'meanings': ['Alex Morrison advocates for a balanced approach to pet care, weighing various factors and avoiding one-size-fits-all solutions.', 'Making informed decisions involves considering the unique needs and circumstances of each pet, rather than following extreme or rigid guidelines.'], 'importance': 'This philosophy promotes adaptability and personalized care in pet wellness, potentially leading to better health outcomes.', 'context': 'It suggests a move away from overly prescriptive or extreme approaches to pet care, towards more nuanced and individualized strategies.', 'confidence': 0.9, 'reasoning': "By emphasizing balance and informed decision-making, Alex Morrison's approach acknowledges the diversity of pets' needs and the importance of flexibility in providing optimal care."}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-6', 'meanings': ["Agent Dale Cooper's diet is tailored to support his specific nutritional needs, focusing on protein and variety.", "High-quality, varied diet is considered essential for maintaining a cat's health and well-being."], 'importance': "This approach to feeding reflects an understanding of the critical role nutrition plays in a pet's overall health.", 'context': 'It highlights the trend towards more specialized and quality-focused pet nutrition, recognizing the importance of diet in preventive health care.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The emphasis on high protein content and dietary variety suggests a commitment to meeting the natural and individual nutritional needs of cats, which is key to their health and vitality.'}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-7', 'meanings': ["Audrey Horne's diet is specifically formulated to support the needs of an active dog, including joint health.", 'Incorporating supplements like glucosamine into a diet for active dogs can help maintain their mobility and overall health.'], 'importance': 'This targeted nutritional approach can enhance the quality of life for active dogs, addressing common health concerns such as joint issues.', 'context': 'It illustrates the growing awareness and application of specialized nutrition in pet care, aimed at supporting specific health needs.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': "By focusing on the nutritional needs of active dogs, including joint health, this approach demonstrates the importance of a tailored diet in supporting a pet's lifestyle and preventing health problems."}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-8', 'meanings': ['Alex Morrison prioritizes hydration in pet care, ensuring easy access to fresh water.', 'Regularly changing water and providing multiple stations encourages pets to stay hydrated, promoting their health.'], 'importance': 'Hydration is a fundamental aspect of pet health, and this practice can prevent a range of health issues.', 'context': 'This attention to water intake and quality reflects a comprehensive approach to pet wellness, recognizing the importance of hydration alongside nutrition and exercise.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': 'The practice of changing water frequently and offering multiple drinking spots is aimed at encouraging pets to drink more water, which is essential for their health and can prevent urinary and kidney issues.'}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-9', 'meanings': ["Audrey Horne's exercise routine is diverse and tailored to her needs, incorporating both physical and mental stimulation.", "Regular, varied exercise is crucial for maintaining a dog's physical health and mental well-being."], 'importance': 'This approach to exercise can lead to a more balanced and healthy lifestyle for dogs, preventing obesity and behavioral issues.', 'context': 'It demonstrates an understanding of the importance of structured, varied physical activity in pet care, aligning with broader trends towards holistic health for pets.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': "By including activities like walks, dog park visits, and agility practice, Audrey Horne's routine addresses the need for both physical exertion and mental engagement, which are vital for a dog's overall health."}, {'chunk_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-10', 'meanings': ["Agent Dale Cooper's activity regimen includes intense, short bursts of play, simulating natural feline behaviors.", "Encouraging natural movement through play and home environment setup is key to a cat's physical and mental health."], 'importance': 'This method of engaging pets in activities that mimic their natural behaviors can improve their well-being and prevent common health issues.', 'context': 'It reflects a broader understanding of the need for pet environments and activities to align with their natural instincts and behaviors for optimal health.', 'confidence': 0.95, 'reasoning': "The focus on intense, short bursts of activity and encouraging natural movement through the home setup is designed to cater to a cat's instinctual patterns, promoting physical health and reducing stress."}], 'patterns': [{'name': 'Comprehensive Pet Wellness', 'description': 'This pattern highlights the multifaceted approach to pet wellness, emphasizing both physical health and emotional well-being through various means.', 'related_inferences': ['d2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-1', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-3', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-4'], 'themes': ['Pet Wellness', 'Emotional Well-being', 'Physical Health'], 'strength': 0.9, 'evidence_count': 3}, {'name': 'Proactive and Personalized Pet Care', 'description': 'This pattern underscores the importance of a proactive and personalized approach to pet care, including preventative measures, tailored diets, and hydration.', 'related_inferences': ['d2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-2', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-5', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-6', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-7', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-8'], 'themes': ['Proactive Care', 'Personalized Diet', 'Hydration'], 'strength': 0.95, 'evidence_count': 5}, {'name': 'Activity and Engagement for Pets', 'description': 'This pattern focuses on the necessity of regular, varied exercise and mental stimulation tailored to the specific needs of pets for their overall well-being.', 'related_inferences': ['d2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-4', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-9', 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644-10'], 'themes': ['Physical Activity', 'Mental Stimulation', 'Tailored Exercise'], 'strength': 0.85, 'evidence_count': 3}], 'insights': [{'headline': 'Pets as Emotional Barometers: Reflecting Human Wellness Trends', 'explanation': "The comprehensive approach to pet wellness, emphasizing both physical health and emotional well-being, mirrors broader societal shifts towards holistic health. This pattern suggests that as humans become more attuned to their own wellness needs, they project these values onto their pets. It's not just about extending lifespans but enhancing the quality of life, recognizing pets as sentient beings with complex emotional landscapes. This shift challenges the traditional view of pets as mere companions or property, elevating their status to that of family members deserving of comprehensive care.", 'pattern_id': 'Comprehensive Pet Wellness', 'non_consensus': True, 'first_principles': True, 'impact_score': 0.9, 'supporting_evidence': ['Rise in pet wellness and therapy services', 'Increased consumer spending on pet health and wellness products', 'Growing recognition of animal sentience in legislation']}, {'headline': 'Customized Pet Care: A New Frontier in Animal Health Innovation', 'explanation': 'The move towards proactive and personalized pet care reflects a deeper understanding of the unique needs of each animal, challenging the one-size-fits-all approach. This pattern is indicative of a broader trend in both human and veterinary medicine towards precision health—tailoring wellness practices and interventions to the individual rather than the population. It underscores a fundamental truth: variability among individuals necessitates a customized approach to achieve optimal health outcomes. This insight could drive innovation in pet health technology, leading to more nuanced and effective care solutions.', 'pattern_id': 'Proactive and Personalized Pet Care', 'non_consensus': True, 'first_principles': True, 'impact_score': 0.8, 'supporting_evidence': ['Emergence of pet DNA testing for tailored health plans', 'Growth in personalized pet nutrition and supplement industry', 'Development of smart pet health monitoring devices']}, {'headline': 'Beyond Play: Activity as a Pillar of Pet Mental Health', 'explanation': 'The emphasis on activity and engagement for pets reveals a critical insight: physical exercise and mental stimulation are not merely for entertainment or to expend energy, but are foundational to mental health and well-being. This challenges the simplistic notion of play as optional, positioning it instead as essential to preventing a range of behavioral and health issues. It reflects a first-principles understanding of well-being that integrates mind and body, suggesting that the mental health of pets is as complex and important as that of humans. This insight could revolutionize pet care practices, prioritizing engagement as a core component of health maintenance.', 'pattern_id': 'Activity and Engagement for Pets', 'non_consensus': True, 'first_principles': True, 'impact_score': 0.85, 'supporting_evidence': ['Studies linking physical activity to reduced anxiety in dogs', 'Innovations in pet toys and games for cognitive development', 'Rising popularity of pet enrichment products and services']}], 'design_principles': [{'principle': 'The system should mirror human wellness trends in pet care, emphasizing holistic health that includes emotional well-being.', 'insight_id': 'Pets as Emotional Barometers', 'action_verbs': ['mirror', 'emphasize'], 'design_direction': 'Holistic pet wellness', 'priority': 0.9, 'feasibility': 0.8}, {'principle': 'The experience must provide personalized pet care solutions, recognizing the unique needs of each animal for optimal health outcomes.', 'insight_id': 'Customized Pet Care', 'action_verbs': ['provide', 'recognize'], 'design_direction': 'Personalized pet care', 'priority': 0.95, 'feasibility': 0.75}, {'principle': 'The system should integrate physical activity and mental stimulation as essential components of pet mental health.', 'insight_id': 'Beyond Play', 'action_verbs': ['integrate', 'consider'], 'design_direction': 'Activity-based mental health', 'priority': 0.9, 'feasibility': 0.85}], 'current_step': 'completed', 'messages': [AIMessage(content='Created 10 chunks using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Generated 10 inferences using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Identified 3 patterns using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Generated 3 insights using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}), AIMessage(content='Created 3 design principles using LangChain output parser', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={})], 'analysis_metadata': {'request_id': 'd2811b32-add0-4a29-be7f-62bf7027a644', 'framework': 'Hybrid (LangGraph + OpenAI Agentic)', 'model': 'gpt-4-turbo-preview', 'orchestration': 'LangGraph', 'function_calling': 'OpenAI Native', 'research_data_s3_path': 'research_data/aas-1-1.txt'}}
